  "query_preview": "..My concern when hoisting a flash flood watch 24\nhours in advance during the monsoon is what happens the day \nbefore and will there be considerable debris clouds starting the\nmorning to mess with the flash flood day forecast. Well that \nhappened this morning, an OH NO moment, with a considerable \ncloud deck and very light showers/virga across the area. This \ncloud deck has been slowly eroding this afternoon with storms \ndeveloping rather quick in the cloud breaks in a rather moist \nand unstable atmosphere. The upper air pattern today is rather \nchaotic today with upper low over wrn TX and an upper high \ncentered over NW AZ bringing a northerly diffluent flow aloft \nacross the area. In addition, there is an MCV west of Organ Pipe\nNtl Mon that is developing storms in western Pima county.\n\nPWATs across the area ranged from 1.3\" near AZ/NM border to \nalmost 2\" over wrn AZ. The unstable atmosphere combined with the\nabove normal moisture will bring an increased flash flooding \nthreat to most of the area. Hourly rainfall rates from some of \nthe strongest storms could exceed 2\"/hour so some of the flash \nflooding could be considerable on a localized basis. Flash Flood\nWatch continues for Graham/Cochise counties thru 9 pm, and for \nPinal, Pima and Santa Cruz counties until 12 am Friday. The \nthreat for severe storms is limited with isolated gusts up to 55\nmph possible. \n\nActivity diminishing overnight with likely considerable cloud\ncover around Friday morning. Likely to have another slow start\nto the convection, with CAMs hitting the Intl border with heavy\nrain. \n\nThunderstorm chances continue daily this weekend and into next\nweek with day to day variability. Ensemble models show the \nupper level ridge becoming centered again over or near the Four \nCorners region next week keeping an active monsoon pattern\nacross the area.\n\nHigh temperatures over the next 7 days will remain below normal.",
